# PLAN-1126 — Python library parity: NumPy and the standard `json` module

Each case has ONE Auto author source. The ordinary AV language entry and the
A2PY `run-py` leg (1110) run the same `### Auto` bytes against the same frozen
oracle. The independent native-Python references (they call NumPy / `json`
directly and never read ACE output) live in
`crates/auto-lang/test/cases/ace-m2/python-1126/`.

Both ends need a NumPy the interpreter can import. AV loads NumPy from the
assembled CPython; the A2PY leg runs `python -I -B` in ISOLATED mode (the
frozen 1042 contract), so its DECLARED interpreter must carry NumPy in its own
site-packages (a user-site install is invisible there). Missing NumPy is a
real `ModuleNotFoundError` (`python.1`), never a pass.

A2PY program output goes through the interpreter's text stream, so on
Windows those lines end in CRLF while the `value:` observation line is raw
bytes ending in LF; the A2PY stream oracles therefore use `compare: "lf"`.
The AV stream oracles stay byte-exact (AV writes LF).

## Case: numpy-json
id: "py.libraries.1126.numpy-json"
tags: ["av-language-parity", "plan-1126"]
entry-mode: "program"

### Auto
path: "main.as"
syntax: "auto-script"

```auto
use.py numpy: arange
use.py builtins: slice
use.py json: loads, dumps

fn main() int {
    var base = arange(6).reshape(2, 3)
    var a = base + arange(3)
    var shape = a.shape
    var row = a[1]
    var selected = row[slice(0, 3, 2)]
    print(shape[0].to(int))
    print(shape[1].to(int))
    print(row[2].to(int))
    print(a.sum().to(int))
    print(selected.sum().to(int))
    print(a.mean().to(float))

    var data = loads("{\"values\":[2,3,5],\"label\":\"雪\",\"meta\":{\"enabled\":true}}")
    var values = data["values"]
    var meta = data["meta"]
    print(values[2].to(int))
    print(meta["enabled"].to(bool))
    var encoded = dumps(data, sort_keys: true, ensure_ascii: false)
    var decoded = loads(encoded)
    print(decoded["label"].to(str))
    print(encoded.to(str))
    print(dumps(decoded, sort_keys: true, ensure_ascii: false).to(str))
    return 0
}
```

